  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1255 AM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA stout ridge will set up across the central US and the Eastern \nSeaboard towards the middle of this week. This will set up a Rex \nBlock pattern with an upper-level low enhancing offshore in the \nwestern Atlantic and advecting southwestwards underneath the ridge \ntowards the Florida Peninsula. With enhanced jet stream dynamics \nalong the eastern side of the ridge, there is increasing \nconfidence that this will cause the strengthening low pressure to \neventually become cut-off by Thursday and Friday as it advects \nacross Florida. The pressure gradient will strengthen quickly on \nWednesday as the low approaches, with a breezier NE flow expected \nversus the lighter flow we have been experiencing lately. Forcing \nfor ascent will increase via positive vorticity advection from the\nlow between Wednesday and Friday, and with deep moisture (PWATs \n1.7-2.0+ inches each day) remaining over the area through this \ntime frame we can expect scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms each day. Therefore, PoPs will be 70-80+% daily for \nthe mid-to-late week period. The cut-off low is expected to be a \ncold core low, which could get interesting in terms of a severe \nthreat as this typically results in steeper lapse rates. However, \ndry air wrapping into the system could lead to some capping and \nresult in a more inhibitive profile for deeper convection. This \nwill be something to monitor over the next several days.\n\nRain chances will continue to remain elevated for next weekend as \nwell due to ongoing deep moisture presence. As the upper low departs \ninto the Gulf later on Friday and into next weekend, low level flow \nwill flip to east-southeasterly and likely shift the highest rain \nchances towards the interior and closer to the Gulf coast but rain \nchances will still be high across the entire region. \n\nOverall, scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected each day through the period. As exact details become more \nclear, QPF confidence will increase which will allow for better \nassessment of any flooding potential. Other than that, no major \nhazards are currently expected other than the usual frequent \nlightning, strong wind gusts, and lower visibilities associated with \nstronger thunderstorm activity. High temperatures each day will be \nin the upper 80s to low 90s. Overnight lows will range from the low \nto mid 70s across SW Florida to the mid to upper 70s along the east \ncoast metro.",
